From 8d606f8643d715f203fc75a428dc39f5ce67a9d5 Mon Sep 28 16:00:00 2026
From: vuln-patch <patch@chainguard.dev>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 16:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix CGP-xpq5-jm7p-884r: reject signature-stripped JWTs when
 keyed

This addresses a CWE-347 signature-stripping authentication bypass in
io.jsonwebtoken.impl.DefaultJwtParser.parse(String jwt). The parser
gated its entire signature-verification block solely on the presence
of a non-empty third (signature) segment in the compact JWT string
(base64UrlEncodedDigest != null). An attacker who strips the
signature segment while keeping the trailing dot (for example
"eyJhbGciOiJub25lIn0.eyJzdWIiOiJhZG1pbiJ9.") makes that check false,
so the entire verification block -- including any configured Key,
key bytes, or SigningKeyResolver -- was skipped, and the parser
returned the attacker-controlled claims or body as authentic. This
is a complete authentication bypass reachable from every public
parse* entry point (parse(String), parse(String, JwtHandler),
parsePlaintextJwt, parseClaimsJwt, parsePlaintextJws, parseClaimsJws),
since they all funnel through the same parse(String) chokepoint.

The root cause is that presence of the signature segment -- entirely
attacker-controlled input -- was the sole gate deciding whether
verification ran, rather than verification being unconditionally
required whenever the parser has verification material configured.

The fix adds a guard at that single parse(String) chokepoint: if the
signature segment is absent (base64UrlEncodedDigest == null) and the
parser has verification material configured (a Key, key bytes, or a
SigningKeyResolver, via the new private isVerificationMaterialConfigured()
helper), it throws a SignatureException before any Header, Claims, or
body is constructed from the untrusted string. Because the guard sits
before object construction and before the pre-existing algorithm/`alg`
branch, it protects all six public entry points uniformly and does not
depend on the attacker-controlled `alg` header value. A parser with no
verification material configured is unaffected and continues to accept
genuinely unsigned tokens (the documented "unsecured JWS" use case).

The regression test, io.jsonwebtoken.SignatureStrippingExploitTest,
exercises all six public entry points and all three verification-
material configurations (Key, key bytes, SigningKeyResolver) with
hand-built stripped-signature tokens, asserting each now throws
MalformedJwtException or SignatureException instead of returning
unverified content; it also adds positive-control tests proving
genuinely unsigned tokens still parse when no verification material
is configured, and that validly-signed tokens -- including through a
caller-defined custom SigningKeyResolver -- still verify correctly.
Five pre-existing tests in JwtParserTest and JwtsTest are updated
because moving the rejection earlier (before body construction)
changes an unsigned-plus-keyed token's exception from the later,
weaker UnsupportedJwtException to the chokepoint's SignatureException;
rejection of the unsigned token is preserved in every case.

CGP: CGP-xpq5-jm7p-884r
Cgp-Finding: CGP-xpq5-jm7p-884r
